ge. Markets and raters alike treat this as a near-certain Democratic hold.", "watch": "A late debate stumble or a cost-of-living-focused Republican surge could narrow the margin, though it would likely need to move more than ten points to threaten the outcome.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Ned Lamont (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Incumbent Democrat Ned Lamont seeks another term against Republican state Senator Ryan Fazio in a state that leans Democratic by about eight points. Recent polling has been uniformly favorable to Lamont: Quinnipiac showed him up 17 points among likely voters in mid-September, the University of New Hampshire had him up 15, and even a Republican-aligned co/efficient survey put him ahead by 7. Aggregated estimates place his lead in the mid-teens.\n\nExpert raters unanimously call the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets price Lamont's chances at 96 to 97 percent. The campaign has centered on cost of living, taxes, and budget policy, with Fazio proposing tax cuts that analysts question, but there is little evidence the contest is competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.994, "margin": 15.28}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.991, "margin": 14.0}]}